    "evidence_text": "Finally, the prediction layer find out the most possible answer choice.\n\nIntroduction\tMany machine learning models in question answering tasks often involve matching mechanism.\n\nThe easiest way of matching is to calculate the cosine\n\nIn this way, QACNN can efficiently extract features cross sentences.\n\n3) The prediction layer which makes the final decision.\n\nThe main contributions of this work are three-fold.\n\nSecond, we introduce attention mechanism into this net.\n\nLastly, our model reaches 79.99% accuracy on the MovieQA dataset which yields top 1 result on this dataset.\n\nQACNN\tIn this question answering task, a reading\n\npassage , a query and several answer choices are given.\n\nFirst, we use embedding layer to transform P, Q, and C into word embedding.\n\nThen the compare layer generates passage-query similarity map INLINEFORM0 and passage-choice similarity map INLINEFORM1 .\n\nThe following part is the main component of QACNN.\n\nIt consists of two-staged CNN architecture.\n\nAfter QACNN Layer, we obtain each choice answer feature.\n\nThe equations here are similar to those previously mentioned.\n\nWe utilized dropout in each CNN layer with dropout rate 0.8.\n\nExperimental Result\tWe mainly focus on the MovieQA dataset to train and evaluate our model.\n\nMovieQA dataset aims to evaluate automatic story comprehension from both video and text.\n\nThe data set consists of almost 15,000 multiple choice question answers.\n\nDiverse information in this dataset like plots, scripts, sub-title and video captions can be used to infer answers.\n\nIn our task, only plot informations are used.\n\nThis challenging dataset is suitable to evaluate QACNN because movie plots are longer than normal reading comprehension task.\n\n3138 QA pairs in the test set.\n\nWe used ensemble model in this dataset.\n\nThe ensemble model consists of eight training runs models with identical structure and hyper-parameter.\n\nIn the test set, as the Table 1.\n\nWe also applied our model to MCTest dataset which requires machines to answer multiple-choice reading comprehension questions about fictional stories.\n\nThe original paper describes that a baseline method uses a combination of a sliding window score and a distance based .\n\nBecause of the restricted training set and development set, we trained our model on MovieQA training set and applied the result to test MCTest dataset.\n\nDiscussion\tQACNN is a powerful network focusing on multiple choice QA task.\n\nIt matches between passage\n\nand choices based on query information.\n\nIn this section, we designed several experiments to test how two-stage mechanism and attention maps impact on our model.\n\nIn this experiment,we focused on the difference between one-stage QACNN and two-stage QACNN.\n\nFor one-stage QACNN, we didn't split an entire passage into sentences.\n\nIn this experiment, our target is to validate the effect of query-based attention in QACNN.\n\nWe\n\nHowever, this modified model would have a deficiency of query information.\n\nThe experiment result is shown on the Table TABREF36 .\n\nto the performance of QACNN.\n\nHowever, sentence-level attention seems to play a more important role.\n\nFigure.\n\nFIGREF31 is the visualization of two attention maps and their corresponding question.\n\nWe used a question from movie, Harry Potter, as an example.\n\nConclusion\tIn this paper, we present an efficient matching mechanism on multiple choice question answering task.\n\nWe introduce two-staged CNN to match passage and choice on word level and sentence level.\n\nIn addition, we use query-based CNN attention to enhance matching effect.\n\nThe power of the model is verified on MovieQA dataset, which yielded the\n\nstate of the art result on the dataset.\n\nIn the future, we are now working on training our model based on our own trained embedding with TF-IDF  weighting.",
